[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Layer-3 routing on the core router between the WAN and Location C (FRR/zebra RIB on router3).

Mechanism: Traffic from Location A (172.16.10.0/24) to Location C (172.18.51.0/24) can be forwarded across R0→R1→R2→R3→R6 using static/kernel routes to 172.18.50/51. However, return traffic from Location C to Location A is dropped at router3 because it has no route for 172.16.10.0/24 (nor 172.16.20.0/24). Router6’s default route points to router3, and router3 responds “Destination Net Unreachable,” causing end‑to‑end failures that appear as flapping to users.

Localization: On router6 we see default via 10.50.1.1 (router3). Pinging 172.16.10.1 from router6 fails with “From 10.50.1.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” proving router3 is the rejecting hop. Router3’s IPv4 table contains only connected nets (10.50.1.0/24, 192.168.100.0/24, 172.25.1.0/24) and kernel routes to 172.18.50/51 via 10.50.1.2; there is no route to 172.16.10.0/24. Pings from pc4 (172.16.10.4) to Location C hosts time out, matching the one‑way blackhole.

Root cause: Missing return routing for Location A networks on router3 (no dynamic routing and no static/default pointing back toward router2/Location A).

Corrective fix: Add reachability for A on router3. Options:
- Preferred: enable RIP v2 on router3/router2 over 192.168.100.0/24 so router3 learns 172.16.10/20 from upstream.
- Or add static routes on router3: “ip route 172.16.10.0/24 192.168.100.1” and “ip route 172.16.20.0/24 192.168.100.1” (or a default to 192.168.100.1).
Either change makes Location C’s return path stable. 
=== END ===